{"id": "121218", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121218", "title": "Concerns", "text": "A number of travel topics are available related to travel concerns.\nBorder crossing.\nDocuments and preparations needed or useful for border crossing:\nCommunication.\nCommunication includes sending and receiving messages.\nEquipment.\nEquipment and other things that travellers might want to carry with them.\nLarge scale travel events.\nSome countries and regions experience periodic travel events, for religious, family or other reasons. Travelling to these regions at these times will present specific challenges for your plans."}
{"id": "121222", "revid": "2196717", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121222", "title": "Preparation", "text": "A number of travel topics are available related to preparing for a trip.\nBorder crossing.\nAre you planning to travel abroad? While some borders can be crossed with no documentation at all, others require a lengthy visa application process.\nEquipment.\nWhat should you carry on your journey?"}
{"id": "121228", "revid": "100394", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121228", "title": "Reasons to travel", "text": " (In reality it is more like a goaty road at evening as your car descends the hill in a cloud of caprine dust, and the lights of Samarkand city shimmer ahead.)\nReaders of these pages will need little selling on reasons to travel, and already have a bank of such travel memories to draw on, and with plans and hopes for more. This index to some travel topics links to advice relevant depending on the reason for the trip you are planning.\nCategories of travellers.\nSee Concerns#Categories of travellers."}
{"id": "121233", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121233", "title": "Rail travel", "text": "Rail travel includes everything from heritage steam trains to high-speed bullet trains, from sightseeing or dinner trains to the convenience of speed as a means of modern transport.\nUnderstand.\nRailroads emerged in the 19th century as the first widely available means of fast land transportation, before the rise of the automobile, and have put their mark on most countries and cities. Canada was unified only by rail until the Trans-Canada Highway was completed in 1961; in Russia, the Trans-Siberian Railway was the only form of land transport from Europe to the Pacific until a Trans-Siberian Highway opened in the early 21st century.\nThe availability and quality of rail infrastructure varies greatly between countries. While countries such as Germany, Japan and China have excellent rail networks both in and between cities, passenger rail service in the United States \u2013 once a world leader in rail transport \u2013 has fallen far behind its road network, and some countries are completely devoid of rail. In the United States, Canada, Australia and New Zealand, long-distance trains are slow, infrequent, expensive and often delayed, meaning that they are primarily intended for tourists with a lot of time and money on their hand to take in the passing scenery at a leisurely pace, rather than an actual practical travel option; if you are pressed for time or on a budget, flying or driving yourself usually works out to be cheaper and faster. In most other developed countries, rail is the primary option for intercity (and intracity) travel where available, although buses may be cheaper. In many developing countries, trains are a relic of the colonial era and are often run-down, slow and unreliable, but are used by many locals as they cost much less than flying or buying a car.\nPublic transportation networks are often made up of regular trains and commuter trains, together with urban rail systems such as metro and streetcars, as well as buses.\nTrains are often the fastest option on distances of 100\u2013800 km. Compared with planes, a rough first estimate is that a train is faster than a competing air-service if it takes three and a half to four hours or less, as getting on a train usually doesn't require being at the station more than some fifteen minutes prior to departure. While trains are slower than planes on long distances, they provide you with a ground-level view of the territory you're visiting, and allows you to stop on the way.\nOn shorter distances, travel to and from the station \u2013 although often centrally located \u2013 adds significant time, and a bus or car can be faster. However, trains are often well integrated with the local transport system, so it is often easy to transfer to a metro, tram or bus (or a bike-sharing bike) for the last distance, while a car driver may struggle to find parking or even to navigate one-way streets.\nTicketing.\nPrices range from cheaper than even a full car \u2013 in some countries that highly subsidise public transport \u2013 to comparable to or even higher than cheap airline-tickets. Especially high speed trains can be as expensive as flying. When comparing prices, note additional fees such as for compulsory seat reservation or for certain forms of payment. In some cases the price depends significantly on the level of service and the time you book. Depending on region, fares may be calculated by distance, speed, type of train or by market demand.\nIf you can, book in advance. In many countries you benefit from a substantial discount, and on popular routes at certain times, longer-distance trains can be fully booked. For some trains, tickets bought aboard incur an extra fee, or there may be no ticket sale on board. Some discounts may depend on the way you buy the ticket. Tickets bought online may have to be exchanged into a physical one at the station or just printed by yourself. Sometimes showing the ticket in your app, browser or as a screenshot is enough.\nMany countries offer passes, allowing several journeys to be made within a region, the most well-known probably being the European rail passes Interrail. Check the price of a pass compared with single or return tickets on the legs you intend to travel \u2013 the passes aren't always cheaper, especially when seat reservation etc. are taken into account.\nMany airlines in North America, Europe and Asia have entered into cooperation with railways to offer through-tickets from the airport to some or any stations of the national network. This can work out to be a steep discount compared to separate tickets or a connecting flight for the last leg of your journey. See Rail air alliances.\nReserving a seat, where optional, will usually incur a small surcharge \u2014 if so, you will have to decide whether or not the train will be empty enough for seats to be readily available (and whether you would give up your seat anyway for somebody in bigger need).\nMany trains have first class seating. This can be affordable in some cases, or very expensive in others. You are paying (typically) for a wider seat and a much emptier compartment. The \"perks\" offered to first class ticket holders are usually fairly minimal (for example, free tea and coffee, better entertainment or newspapers). Do not travel in first class unless you have a ticket or other permission to do so. In some countries (such as Belgium), pregnant women have first class access at no extra cost.\nOvernight services may offer berths. These must usually be booked in advance and incur a surcharge, often very moderate compared to a hotel night.\nWhen to travel.\nIf possible, try to avoid commuter rush hours. For local trains, this typically means travel on trains due to arrive at a reasonably large town or city between (roughly) two hours before and one hour after the traditional start of work, and departing between an hour before and two hours after the traditional end of the workday. Still, rush hour trains tend to be more frequent, and if you don't mind standing, you're more likely to have good connections in peak periods.\nIn western countries, trains are often crowded on Friday evenings and Sunday afternoons. Some lines, especially high speed lines, are frequently used by \"weekend commuters\" and thus particularly prone to overcrowding on Friday evenings and Sunday evenings. Especially local services may be reduced in Sundays and public holidays, and services may even be non-existent on some public holidays.\nThe days immediately prior to \"family holidays\" (Thanksgiving in North America, Christmas in Europe, the end of Ramadan in Muslim countries, Chinese New Year in China, Golden Week in Japan, Easter Week in Latin America, etc.) are especially prone to crowding as well as higher prices.\nSometimes there are a few discount tickets or special discounts apply for certain days, times of the year or hours of the day, when demand is low. You may want to plan ahead to take advantage of the discounts and less crowded services.\nServices in the late evening or early morning are often sparse and may not be as safe. Local services may have a night surcharge. In countries with flexible pricing, taking the \"red-eye\" on a long-distance service may save you considerable money, just as it does in aviation.\nOvernight services usually have sleeper cabins or \"couchettes\", but often also normal seats. Travelling overnight may save you a hotel night and a travel day. Intermediate stations may have odd departure and arrival times (read: in the small hours of the night) and some of them may be skipped altogether.\nAt the station.\nTrain stations tend to be centrally located, especially if built before automobiles became common, Some newer stations, such as typically for high-speed lines, may be farther away, but well-integrated with the local transport infrastructure, such as having a metro station below the train terminal. Some airports have dedicated train stations, usually served by a local train to the main station.\nSome services require you to validate your ticket before boarding your train, on boarding or before entering the platform area. There are often machines for that on the platform or in the train. Fare inspectors tend to be humourless, and little impressed by any excuse you may have for not having a valid ticket. If your ticket isn't on paper, make sure that your device isn't out of battery when the ticket is to be inspected.\nBe on time, or early. At many stations, if you have bought the tickets in advance, turning up 10 min before departure is more than enough, but allow more time if the departure station is large and unfamiliar. Longer distance (inter-city) trains often lock the doors 30\u201360 seconds before departure. Sometimes an earlier arrival is necessary, for example in China where there are security and identity checks before boarding, or for the Channel Tunnel between England and the continent, where border checks are done before boarding.\nFamiliarise yourself with the layout of the station. Usually tracks or platforms are continuously numbered starting with one, but there are exceptions and odd layouts are not unheard of. Sometimes a part of the tracks is subterranean, which may or may not be indicated on your ticket. Some tickets don't indicate the platform, but leave it to you to check the displays at the station \u2013 and sometimes the track has changed since the ticket was printed.\nStand well back from the platform edge, except when you are actually boarding, behind any yellow lines. This applies especially when express or through trains pass the station. They can generate a lot of turbulence as they pass. Face the platform edge if you are wearing a backpack, so the pack won't be caught by a moving train.\nIf you have anything with wheels or rollers with you, keep an eye on it, make sure the brakes (if any) are engaged and position it parallel to the tracks. This will minimise the risk of rolling on platforms which slope slightly towards the tracks.\nDo not cross the tracks except at an authorised, controlled crossing or under the direction of staff. Look both ways before crossing, even if warning devices are operating. Do not cross against warning devices.\nMany trains call for very brief intervals at smaller stations, sometimes as short as 30 seconds. Have all your baggage at hand and be prepared to board quickly. Tuck away loose pieces of baggage like drinks, maps, guidebooks and coats \"before\" the train arrives to ensure a smooth boarding procedure.\nFind out if the class or car you are boarding is in the front, middle or rear of the train and position yourself on the platform accordingly. Ask the staff on the platform. In some countries, stations are divided into zones and diagrams show you what zone your coach number corresponds to; for Japan and China's high-speed trains, even the exact positions of the doors are painted on the platform.\nAt busy stations you may need to make sure that you board the right train: there may be another one at the same platform just a few minutes before yours.\nBoarding.\nIf your car doesn't stop where you expected it to, you may have to hurry \u2013 don't run if you don't have to, but make sure you get onboard with the other passengers.\nBefore boarding a train, \"wait\", on either side of the doors, until all passengers getting off have alighted. Trains will \"not\" depart if there are still people queuing to get on, even if it means they're a minute late, unless there is an explicit rule spelled on the ticket stating that \"the train may skip scheduled stations in case of delay\" \u2013 such an exception can be very frequent services at rush hour, where a few seconds delay can cause many following trains to be delayed, but in this case the next train will not be far behind.\nGive way to encumbered passengers, such as people with lots of bags, people with children and people in wheelchairs. Offer them a hand if you can if they're struggling.\nDo not enter or leave any train that is moving or while the doors are closing. If someone is stuck in the door, alert the guard. Many modern trains have a safety interlock, which prevents the driver starting the train if the doors are not fully closed and locked, but they can fail. If the train does begin to move, operate the emergency stop/door release lever/button. Do this only in an emergency, as once the train is stopped this way it may need to be inspected from end to end.\nMany trains, particularly commuter services, have some device to see if somebody is still trying to board or alight. Often this comes in the form of a photo sensor in the door, that may be triggered by people standing to close to the door. Get away from the door once you're on board, as you might otherwise trigger the sensor, keeping the doors from shutting and thus holding up the train.\nOn board.\nTuck away your baggage as much as you can. Don't let it block the aisle or the seats for other passengers. There may be baggage racks at the ends of carriages for larger items, or overhead space for smaller bags. Sit down at your seat. If you want to check out the rest of the train, such as going to the restaurant car, wait with that until everybody has settled.\nIf you don't have a seat reservation and the train is lightly loaded, you can usually just sit anywhere. Exceptions include compartments for a more expensive ticket class and seats marked as reserved. Whether you should sit down close to strangers or not depends on local culture as on any public transport, although people on long-distance services may be more prone to make an acquaintance.\nIf few seats remain, seats can be reserved and reserved seats aren't marked in any way you can see, you may want to ask staff, if available. Otherwise, you may choose to just sit down, risking to have to move as the one who reserved it has made their way there. Alternatively, you can try to keep out of the way at first, and choose among those remaining. If all seats are taken, just try to find a safe place to stand or squat. Do not occupy the roof, the toilet, the baggage racks or the space under the seats or tables.\nAmenities.\nTrains offer a wide variation of amenities. Some trains offer airline style entertainment systems, Wi-Fi and power sockets. Others offer very little by way of facilities, some not even toilets.\nStandards of train toilets vary widely, from very basic or even utterly unhygienic to spotless and modern. There may be an accessible toilet.\nSome trains have a compartment for families with children, perhaps with a playing corner, children's books and a suitably equipped toilet. There may be baby formula heating equipment. On trains without such a compartment, children may still be given some entertainment. Carry your own anyway.\nFood.\nAlmost all railways allow you to bring your own food and eat it en route. For money-conscious travellers, and travellers with dietary restrictions, this is often the best option. Choose food that won't make a mess and clean up after yourself. See also Packed food.\nIn some countries people may enter the train to sell food. It may also be sold through windows or on the platform during a (short) stop. This is usually cheap but at your own risk of travellers' diarrhea. In high income countries these people are usually railway employees or officially licensed, with their selection of food and drinks mostly limited and overpriced.\nIn most long distance trains there is some form of dining car (labelled any number of terms) with food ranging from microwaved junk-food to freshly cooked local specialities depending on the train. Of course expect to pay more for smaller portions than you would for comparable food outside a train.\nIn some trains (usually the \"premium\" category i.e. high-speed first class) you can get food served at your seat. If it is included in your fare, don't expect too much. Sometimes you will have to pay more than in the dining car. In China, you can use an app to order food from restaurants at some intermediate stations en route to your destination on long-distance trains, and it will be delivered to your seat when the train makes its stop. In Finland you can likewise use an app to get food delivered to your seat, but the selection will be that of the train; on overnight trains, you can order breakfast to your cabin. \nIn Japan, \"ekiben\" (\u99c5\u5f01), essentially a boxed meal meant for consumption on trains, is sold at major train stations and on board long-distance trains. Many cities take pride in their \"ekiben\", which often feature local specialities. The \"ekiben\" tradition was exported to Taiwan under Japanese colonial rule, and it continues today in the form of the Taiwan Railway Bento (\u81fa\u9435\u4fbf\u7576), which in its most traditional form consists of a stewed pork chop on rice with a braised hard boiled egg, some tofu and vegetables.\nSleeper services.\nIn many countries there will be sleeper services at night. While you will usually pay a premium for the accommodation, you also possibly save on a hotel and \"lose\" less time in transit even if the total travel-time might sometimes be higher (in some countries sleeper-trains have lower maximum speeds than regular trains). Consider investing in a \"couchette\" or sleeper compartment, which are often cost-competitive with lodgings for the night. A couchette cabin has around 6 beds for sleeping and no other facilities, while a full-fledged sleeper will have two to four beds and possibly bathing facilities like a sink or shower. \nTo avoid the need to change trains in the middle of the night, many railroads split up and join trains during the trip. Triple check you are in the right car, especially if you are unfamiliar with the local script.\nBecause of the noise and the shaking of the train, the quality of the sleep may not be as good as in a real bed. Some people, however, sleep better while on a moving train. Having several persons in the same tiny room could also make the atmosphere smelly.\nIf no bathing facility is available, you may not feel comfortable the next day of your travel, and you may need to find facilities elsewhere.\nYou will probably arrive before check-in time of your hotel. If you don't want to make a return journey just to leave your luggage there, you may want to leave your luggage in lockers for the day \u2013 luckily, train stations often have them.\nEspecially if your destination is a second-rate or minor city along the route, your arrival time may be in the middle of the night or in the early morning. See Arriving in a new city#Get in for some tips.\nBaggage.\nRail lines usually have no explicit weight or size limits for baggage, with some few exceptions. However, they assume that passengers can carry their baggage with them. While suitcases sometimes need to be stored elsewhere, a carry-on bag can be stored in the overhead storage, or at the seat.\nOn some trains the overhead racks are cramped and there may be no other storage space. On such trains, especially if they are crowded, carrying a lot of luggage can be a problem.\nMotorail.\nSome long-distance trains allow you to bring your car on the train with you, in which you load your car onto special carriages and travel in the regular passenger carriages. This is today most common in continental Europe, but is also available on a few select routes in the United States, Canada, Australia and Argentina. \nCope.\nRail travellers might encounter timetables and information being available in local languages only, poorly translated information or hard to understand ticketing requirements.\nStay safe.\nTrains are generally an extremely safe mode of transportation. This is especially true for high-speed railways. The Japanese Shinkansen has not recorded a single on-board fatality in 50 years of operation (although one passenger was killed in 1995 when the train started while their hand was stuck in a passenger door). Even though it might sound counter-intuitive, high speed trains have a better safety record than \"normal\" trains. All trains that go above the general speed limit (160 km/h in continental Europe, 79 mph in the USA, 125 mph in Great Britain) are equipped with continuous in-cab signalling, which interacts with the trains systems and automatically applies the brakes if the driver exceeds the speed limit or is at risk of passing a signal at danger. This makes accidents caused by driver error drastically more unlikely. Furthermore, high speed lines are built without level crossings and part of the process of upgrading a \"normal\" line is removing or drastically reducing the number of level crossings. In some countries high speed lines are even fenced off or elevated, thus almost eliminating the possibility of something or somebody getting on the tracks.\nIn a few developing countries like Egypt where rail infrastructure and rolling stock are inadequate and unmaintained, rail travel can often be dangerous, and accidents can be lethal. You should consider other transportation when travelling in these countries.\nThat being said, there are some ways to increase your personal safety:\nThe major risk factor for the average passenger, is crime on board, and at the stations. Most trains in developed countries are fairly safe as far as petty criminal activity is concerned. However, take reasonable precautions against theft wherever you are. Ensure that your baggage is kept in your sight at all times. If you have your own compartment, lock the door from the inside when sleeping, preferably with your own lock.\nEspecially pickpocketing and molestation do occur at stations and on crowded trains, though usually no more than in other crowded situations."}
{"id": "121249", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121249", "title": "Rhosneigr", "text": "Rhosneigr is a village in the county of Anglesey in North Wales. It has less than 750 residents but is popular with visitors for its sandy beaches on the Anglesey coast, local bird life and idyllic countryside views.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nRhosneigr is an easy 30 minute drive from Bangor. Coming from that direction, you'll need to take the A55 toward Caergybi/Holyhead and then the A4080/A5 exit to Rhosneigr/Aberffraw/Bryngwran.\nBy train.\n is on the Holyhead line with connections to Wrexham, Shrewsbury, Birmingham International, Crewe and Cardiff.\nBy bus.\nBus 25 from Aberffraw and Holyhead and Bus 45 from Llangefni"}
{"id": "121250", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121250", "title": "Bullhead City", "text": "Bullhead City is in Northern Arizona.\nUnderstand.\nBullhead City is across the Colorado River from Laughlin, Nevada, known for its many casinos. \nSpirit Mountain in the distance was known as the origin of life on earth for the Mojave people who once occupied the area. \nIn 1864 a landing for steamboats from Yuma was established here called Hardyville as it was the furthest north that the river was continuously navigable. But over time other landings as well as railroad crossings stole commerce away until Hardyville basically vanished. \nBy the 1970s, vitality returned with the building of Davis Dam and Lake Mohave. Between Bullhead City and Laughlin on the Nevada side of the river, the area boomed, especially with snowbirds escaping from their wintry domiciles elsewhere.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nArizona State Hwy 68 bisects Bullhead City, connecting to Kingman and eventually Laughlin to the west. Bullhead City can be reached from Las Vegas via US 95 to NV state Route 163, or from Kingman via AZ state Route 68."}
{"id": "121256", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121256", "title": "Daugai", "text": "Daugai is a town in Dz\u016bkija the southern region of Lithuania. It's on the banks of Lake Did\u017eiulis and is a holiday resort, with a population in 2020 of 928. \nGet in.\nBy road from Vilnius is 89 km, follow A4 then branch west on Highway 128.\nBuses from Vilnius run three times a day, taking 80 min, and continue to Alytus and Marijampol\u0117. Two buses a day run from Kaunas, taking 2 hr 15 min via Prienai and Alytus. For tickets and times see Autobusubiletai.\n is on Daug\u0173 g at the north edge of town. A statue of an armoured axeman stands nearby, but it doesn't mean the bus service is axed. \nGet around.\nTown is small and walkable, but you could use a bike to explore the lake shores. The former Alytus-Var\u0117na railway has been turned into a cycleway.\nDrink.\nDaugai has no bars or nightclubs.\n No hotels here, but several in Alytus. \nConnect.\nAs of March 2024, Daugai and its approach highways have 4G from Bite and Telia, but no signal from Tele2. 5G has not reached this area."}
{"id": "121258", "revid": "2196717", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=121258", "title": "Kalasha Valleys", "text": "The Kalasha Valleys are in Pakistan.\nUnderstand.\nThe Kalasha Valleys are home to the waning and fascinating Kalasha people. About 40 km from the town of Chitral live the famous Kafir Kalash tribe. They are known in the world over for their pagan traditions and their love for dance and music.\nThe Kalash, the smallest distinct ethnoreligious community in Pakistan, are the last remaining people in the Hindu Kush who continue to follow an ancient pre-Islamic faith that was once widespread throughout the mountain region. Today, they live primarily in three remote valleys\u2014Bumburet (Mumuret), Rumbur (Rukmu), and Birir (Biriu)\u2014which branch south from the Chitral River.\nGet in.\nIt takes about 8 to 10 hours drive from Islamabad to Chitral, one can also reach Chitral by air from Peshawer Airport.\nThe more popular route by land is from Gilgit- taking 14 hours if all the jeeps on the 5-7 steps of the route are co-ordinated. This route is considered the safer and more scenic.\nAnother land route is from Peshawar. Jeeps leave Chirtal Mayoon (600 rs) at 4am in Khyber Bazaar and take 14 hours; crossing over the beautiful Lowri pass. As of July 2009 the Lowri Pass has been reopened after the violence in Swat and Malakhand which spilt over onto the pass had been eliminated. Due to a heavy police and army presence, the route has become safe for tourists. It is said that the works on the Lowri tunnel will recommence by September resulting in an opening before years end- should no incident occur.\nAs regards the Kalash Valleys, jeeps are available in the morning and mid afternoon (8o rs or 1000 for 'special taxi') which run via Ayun. Roads to Valley are unmetalled and on one side of the jeep are Hindukush and on the other side is the river. A 200rs toll applies to enter the valleys. Most people only visit central valley Bambore at where there are many hotels &amp; other amenities. Rumbur is less often visited but has a higher proportion of kalash and a greater historical significance to the Kalash people (but watch out for the converted Afghan-Kalash at the northern end of the valley who, while not dangerous, are unfriendly to foreigners and enemies to the kalash as the result of them squatting on Kalash lands).\nSee.\nKalash Festivals.\nThe Kalash are ancient tribe and have a religion and culture of their own, their culture is unique as well as amazing one. The Kalash is famous for their festivals. Locally brewed mulberry wine is drunk in copious quantities. The Kalash People celebrate three main festivals in a year.\nOrganized to pay thanks to Almighty on the arrival of spring season.\nPay homage to Almighty because of grapes and other ripe fruit. They prepare wine, cheese, corns etc.\nIt is a winter festival celebrated to welcome the New Year. It is celebrated by feasting, drinking and merry making. It is thanksgiving function to Almighty. Winter is full of snow, rain and cold wind. People rejoice with wine, fruit, assemble for dancing, singing and enjoy every event and moment.\nDo.\nVisit the Kalasha people, the inhabitants. Also do a travel trek around the area with a local guide.\nBuy.\nKalasha dresses\nEat.\nLocal food\nDrink.\nThough wine is haram, the Kalasha drink it as part of their culture, and the Kalasha often serve it to foreigners.\nSleep.\nAt local Kalasha guest houses. Hotels can be also found in the northeast of the valleys."}
